lookup cache: a compact record of each thread's cached blocks, behind a switch

Every thread records which translated blocks it has put in its own lookup caches, by page of
guest code, so that when a page changes it can drop exactly those. The record is a map from
page to a hash set of block addresses. A hash set has at least 16 buckets of 16 bytes and
doubles from there, so the record costs about 60 bytes for each block a thread has cached, and
every thread has its own. With much translated code and many busy threads that is gigabytes:
once everything unused was out of the way (0005 to 0007), 2.4 GB of Arknights: Endfield's
11.3 GB was FEX's heap, 1.4 GB of it in eight threads.

With FEX_COMPACTBLOCKRECORDS=1 a page instead has a sorted array of 32-bit offsets (the
block's address less the page's; a block is recorded under every page it covers, so the
offset can be negative). Finding a block is a binary search, adding one moves at most a page's
worth of offsets, and invalidating a range walks the same ordered map as before. A block more
than 2 GB from a page it is recorded under goes into a short list on the side; there are
normally none. The original record is untouched and is what runs with the switch off, which
is the default.

Test with 32 threads that each ran 48,000 blocks: FEX's heap 103 MB with the switch off, 56 MB
with it on. tests/runtime/code-rewrite.c checks with either setting that code which is
rewritten in place, protected and rewritten, or freed and replaced never runs in its old form
on any of 8 threads.

